## Structure (concentric layers)

```
src/
├── domain/                       ← innermost: pure business rules, no framework
│   ├── entities/                 ← enterprise entities
│   ├── services/                 ← domain services (pure logic)
│   └── repositories/             ← repository interfaces (abstractions)
├── application/                  ← orchestrates domain, depends on ports
│   ├── services/                 ← application services
│   ├── ports/                    ← interfaces (DI tokens here)
│   └── dto/                      ← request shapes
├── infrastructure/               ← adapters that implement ports
│   ├── persistence/              ← ORM-backed repositories
│   ├── web/                      ← controllers + modules
│   └── messaging/                ← event publishers
└── presentation/                 ← NestJS bootstrapping
    ├── app.module.ts             ← root module
    └── main.ts                   ← bootstrap
```

**The onion rule:** dependencies point only inwards. `presentation` → `infrastructure` → `application` → `domain`. Nothing in an inner layer imports from an outer layer.

Domain services contain pure business logic with zero framework imports. Application services orchestrate domain services through ports. Infrastructure implements those ports with concrete technology.

Repositories are currently in-memory. To move to a real database, add a class implementing the domain repository interface and swap it in the module's provider list.

